From: Platina Linux contributors
Subject: [PATCH] media: qcom: camss: provide SDM660 PHY clock rate tables

SDM660 requires csiX_phy >= csiphyX_timer. The selection logic is present
but skips csiX_phy because its resource clock_rate table contains only 0.
Observed during a real Platina IMX363 stream: timer 100MHz, csi0_phy 19.2MHz;
the sensor frame counter increments, and CSID internal testgen reaches RAM,
but external sensor data produces no VFE SOF. Give the PHY the same existing
supported levels as its timer so the intended selection logic runs.

Reference: AngeloGioacchino Del Regno, tested Sony Xperia XA2 SDM630 series,
https://lkml.iu.edu/2010.2/01479.html
This restores the requirement from the original SDM630/660 CAMSS port.

--- a/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss.c
+++ b/drivers/media/platform/qcom/camss/camss.c
@@ -830,7 +830,7 @@
 				{ 0 },
 				{ 0 },
 				{ 100000000, 200000000, 269333333 },
-				{ 0 } },
+				{ 100000000, 200000000, 269333333 } },
 		.reg = { "csiphy0", "csiphy0_clk_mux" },
 		.interrupt = { "csiphy0" },
 		.csiphy = {
@@ -849,7 +849,7 @@
 				{ 0 },
 				{ 0 },
 				{ 100000000, 200000000, 269333333 },
-				{ 0 } },
+				{ 100000000, 200000000, 269333333 } },
 		.reg = { "csiphy1", "csiphy1_clk_mux" },
 		.interrupt = { "csiphy1" },
 		.csiphy = {
@@ -868,7 +868,7 @@
 				{ 0 },
 				{ 0 },
 				{ 100000000, 200000000, 269333333 },
-				{ 0 } },
+				{ 100000000, 200000000, 269333333 } },
 		.reg = { "csiphy2", "csiphy2_clk_mux" },
 		.interrupt = { "csiphy2" },
 		.csiphy = {
